e live in  countries that are either neutral in  this conflict or are in fact supporting  russia  and so the west is itself invigorated  but it has not yet  made the case sufficiently to the rest  of the world that the stakes here are  stakes that matter for them and i think  that's really important so the challenge  i think will be for the west to match  its own  renewed sense of purpose and strength  by convincing the rest of the world that  they too have a stake in this and that a  rules-based order is important  across the world before the war in  ukraine russia's army was seen as  powerful capable modernized  what we have seen in ukraine points to  the opposite and that isn't just  important for the war itself and  russia's struggles in ukraine this will  also have long lasting effects on the  way that the world sees russia  russian military power russian political  power diplomatic power all of it has  taken a huge hit russia is coming out of  this war significantly weakened its  economy is in serious trouble its  diplomacy looks fumbling and inept it  hasn't been able to stop countries like  finland and sweden from seeking  membership in nato and that i think is a  long lasting piece of reputational  damage to the russian state what russia  has done in ukraine is to use its  nuclear weapons as a kind of shield  behind which to wage its war inside the  country and it is said to the west  if you intervene directly if you get  your forces involved and they get in our  way  you will risk a conventional war that  may escalate to nuclear weapons  and that has so far worked russia has  used nuclear weapons to keep the west  out of the conflict and i think what  that does is make nuclear weapons more  important to global security than they  have been in many years and countries  like china may be watching this for  lessons and they may be thinking well if  we want to invade taiwan perhaps we can  also use nuclear weapons as a shield to  keep america and its allies  out of that war and keep them from  intervening so i think that's going to  be the lesson that many countries draw  the way in which nuclear weapons can  still be used not for war fighting  purposes  or anything else but it's a kind of  deterrent behind which you can conduct a  military offensive there are a trio of  effects that you can now see on the  world economy and some of them are  unexpected all of them are far-reaching  start with the most short-term effect  which is that the war has amplified the  inflation problem that was already  rippling through the world economy  because russia is a big energy exporter  and because ukraine is a big exporter of  some commodities you've seen a huge  price shop go through  energy and commodity markets and some  people  think it could even  trigger a recession in the u.s as the  federal reserve has to put its foot on  the brakes harder to try and get  inflation under control the second  big effect uh we can see three months in  to the war in ukraine is on supply  chains and that will take a year or two  to become fully apparent and companies  once again have found themselves  unable to fill shop  shelves with  products struggling to find components  and their manufacturing processes in  chaos but the bottom line is this great  redesign of globalization and global  supply chains is now more fully underway  as a result of of the war  the final big change  that's  visible at three months after the  conflict began  relates to sanctions so western  governments have fired a bazooka at  russia's economy they tried to cut off  its financial system its central bank  and quite a lot of the trade russia does  with the outside world  the big implication of that relates to  china you can guarantee that china is  putting together a more cohesive plan  for protecting its financial system and  economy  from western sanctions that might  actually end up being the single biggest  economic implication  of the war in russia and ukraine well  the world has changed absolutely in a  fundamental way  and i don't think we are through this  story at all i think we're in the first  part russia has itself has changed  dramatically uh in this three months it  would have been very hard to imagine  three months ago  that um some of russia's best and  brightest would have fled the country  would have to flee the country uh in  days after the um the uh invasion of  ukraine  that all independent media would be shut  down  in russia i think it made russia's  regime much more brittle  it  speeded up  the moment when we might see the  collapse of this regime and with it  possibly further disintegration  of russia itself we've also discovered  that ukraine the country that many  people thought was  chaotic and corrupt and not quite a  country  few people maybe could even um point its  borders  on map  let alone history has proved itself to  be a genuinely viable and strong country  that has come together his people have  come together in an extraordinary way  and certainly nobody could imagine that  the war would go on  for three months  and that the there is actually a  possibility of ukraine's victory  thank you for watching for all our  coverage on the war in ukraine please  click on the link and don't forget to  subscribe
 

